I always love your newsletter., podcasts and all the content.

15
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,320
So it's what an honor to be here with you.

16
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:39,840
Oh, thank you so much, man.

17
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:53,840
Well, so I've had several ex Tinder employees on the podcast, but I'm super excited to get you on, because I haven't gotten to talk to anyone who's been in the trenches the last few years, and Tinder has done a lot of cool stuff on the monetization front.

18
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,920
So I wanted to dive into a project I know you worked a lot on, and that's AI driven pricing.

19
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:01,360
So what was the problem?

20
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:03,920
What were you trying to solve, and how did things go?

21
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:04,320
Yeah.

22
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:05,680
Yeah, I'm very excited.

23
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:13,040
So let me start with the core problem because that's really, you know, shaped everything we build at Tinder.

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,240
So the user problem is decision overload, simplified to put that way.

25
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:31,920
So as you might know, Tinder has a lot of purchase options for multiple subscription tiers and then under each tiers there's multiple plans like weekly, monthly and then we have a lot of auto card products.

26
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:32,960
So it's great, right?

27
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,520
Because we have so many products for you to choose from.

28
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,800
However, we talked to users and we noticed two things, at least.

29
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:50,320
One is some users they bought the Platinum, the highest subscription tier we offer to everyone, simply because that's the most expensive one.

30
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:55,520
So their belief is that, well, I'm willing to pay the most and that I'm going to get the best.

31
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,160
So I'm going to buy Platinum, which is great.

32
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:05,840
However, we noticed some of the users who bought Platinum, they only use the features under our second tier, GOAT.

33
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:11,120
So they'll realize, oh, actually, you bought something you didn't even fully use.

34
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,680
You could have just bought our GO subscription tier to save you money.

35
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:20,080
Or maybe another issue can be like we can do a better job to educate users.

36
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:22,400
Hey, there is other feature from Platinum.

37
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:23,760
You should take advantage of them.

38
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:24,880
You haven't used them yet.

39
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:32,000
Another thing we noticed is some users they didn't buy anything simply because they were overwhelmed.

40
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,240
It's like, oh, there are so many options.

41
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:35,200
I don't want to pick.

42
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:37,280
And then also reality we learn is.

43
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:39,920
maybe David, you have your own experience.

44
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,400
When you look particularly at revenue cat specialised in paywall, right?

45
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:50,480
So one common mistake a lot of companies make is they describe all the features or benefits.

46
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:52,240
The benefits are better than features, yes.

47
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:55,920
However, do you think users are going to reset and make a decision?

48
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:58,240
No, that's what we thought users were going to do.

49
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:05,840
Okay, let's compare Tingers Plus, Tingers Glow, Tingers Planum and then compare the other car and then make a decision.

50
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,320
No, users make a decision within a second.

51
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,920
So it's our job to help them to make decisions better.

52
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:18,000
So that's from the basic user problem is like decision overload and then so it's overwhelming.

53
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:25,440
And then from the business problem is of course that leads to relatively lower conversion, right?

54
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:31,840
Because for some users who wanted to buy but they were overwhelmed, they decide not to buy and then that lower conversion.

55
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:36,320
So that's a classic case of misalignment incentives.

56
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:39,600
So we wanted to maximize revenue by our own resources.

57
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:44,080
But our UI experience made it harder for you to make a confident decision.

58
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,640
Yeah, and I mean, Tinder famously.

59
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:47,840
I mean, this is why I have you on.

60
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:59,680
And so many people in the industry have talked about Tinder because it's led the way in these hybrid monetization, but having three different levels of plan and then you all tested out a fourth level of plan.

61
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,160
We'll talk about that toward the end, hopefully, if we have time.

62
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:03,920
But it does get super confusing.

63
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:08,000
And then you have the super the boosts and the other in-app purchases and things like that.

64
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:15,360
So then what did you do to actually solve this that and to make it more accessible to folks and getting people to the right plan.

65
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:21,520
So that was a challenging, but luckily we had a brilliant machine learning team.

66
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:38,320
So when I talked to the team members and then they told me, hey, we can try to build ML models and then use that to predict users' winners to pay and then we can surface the best product they might most likely to buy.

67
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:44,960
So that's our solution because based on the insight we tal talk about is like people, customers don't need a lot of options.

68
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:46,000
They need the right one.

69
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,760
Think about like Netflix.

70
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,320
You know how many users like, oh my god, I don't know what to watch.

71
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:52,080
There's so many choices.

72
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,320
They end up spending hours scrolling and then not making watch anything.

73
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,360
That's why Netflix has something like top picks for you, right?

74
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:05,280
Based on your previous behavior and your ratings, they predict what you're most likely to watch.

75
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:06,480
So solve that problem.

76
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:07,520
So very similar.

77
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:18,960
So this is a huge moment for us, I think, particularly even for Tinder for the industry is we shift from stale pricing to like dynamic pricing.

78
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,960
So we don't show the same paywall, same product to all the users.

79
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:30,880
So think about, let's say, David, you are winning to buy Platinum, why should we show you Plus, right?

80
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:33,360
I mean, that's a problem not just from a business perspective.

81
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:41,120
We won't be able to maximize the revenue and then we can use that to reinvest to make product better for you.

82
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:46,080
Another problem is you don't get to invest enjoy all the best benefits we can offer, right?

83
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:48,240
Simply because you chose the plus.

84
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:51,120
So that's what the solution comes with.

85
00:06:51,520 --> 00:07:01,440
We use machine learning, we train the model and then we eventually release a model for that to predict and serve the best SKU for customers.

86
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:02,720
How do you test that?

87
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:04,880
Like what was the AB test?

88
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:15,520
It was just the standard kind of deterministic, normal, everyone gets this paywall at this part of the flow and then everyone gets that paywywall at that part of the flow.

89
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:23,520
And did you just do an AB test to prove that the ML decision model was better than what you had before?

90
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:25,440
Yes, full on, yes.

91
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:33,520
But obviously to reduce the risk, we couldn't just start it from all the paywalls, right?

92
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,280
Because we have a lot of different paywalls.

93
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:45,120
And then at the same time, that's very expensive to and it takes a lot of time and then effort to train the model to test the model.

94
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,280
So we start with something small.

95
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:53,440
So we test with a couple of features and then just start with not all the scope.

96
00:07:53,600 --> 00:08:06,560
So we can see, hey, based on this machine model to historically, you know, like a version a control will be the same, you know, like as we used to show the paywall and the products to the new one.

97
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:07,680
This is dynamic.

98
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:16,080
We will change based on the basically when they show user, let's say for David and then our paywall will ask the email.

99
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,560
The ML model, hey, which paywall should we show?

100
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,080
And then the paywall will decide, okay, which product we should recommend to David.

101
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:25,120
And then that's how we test the treatment.

102
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:29,200
And then we'll be able to measure the conversion and then the total revenue.

103
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:30,720
And how did it do?

104
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:31,440
It's great.

105
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:31,680
Yeah.

106
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:40,080
So, based on our prediction, I think it's definitely multi million dollars annual increase for Tinder.

107
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:45,440
I also want to clarify, you know, for that, it's like, it's not just like maximum revenue.

108
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:46,800
It's like an evil business plan.

109
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,720
No, it's like the money we're going to use, we can reinvest to the product, right?

110
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:59,120
So we can improve our user experience and then build more like benefits for customers.

111
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:07,280
That's why when I talk to a lot of startup funders, I always advise them, hey, don't wait to think about your monetization strategy.

112
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:10,160
You know, don't feel bad to charge users.

113
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:18,080
They wanted to pay you and they deserve it because you're going to provide better experience for them, not mention to compete with your competitors.

114
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:20,560
Comparators and they'll be able to move faster and sooner.

115
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,400
What about countermetrics?

116
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:33,200
Was there anything you were watching to make sure things didn't go south in retention or user experience, you know, that people weren't happy with the plans that they were presented?

117
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:41,680
Tinger has a really good process in place and that we are required to also measure our countermetrics.

118
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:46,800
This is great because, you know, you can't just focus on one area.

119
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:54,480
For example, for this case, we can't just focus on one area the first time conversion and then only the revenue amount, right?

120
00:09:54,720 --> 00:10:03,360
So we have to measure, hey, let's say David based on our model, maybe prior to that, he would buy a plus, now he bought Platinum.

121
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,760
So we want to check, hey, is David going to come back?

122
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:08,000
Is David going to buy Platinum again?

123
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,280
He's going to cancel.

124
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:19,840
So we definitely measure this long term success metrics to make sure this model really served correctly, because that's not something we wanted to continue to work on.

125
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:22,240
Like most product features, right?

126
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:24,560
It's not like launch and down, that's it.

127
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:27,200
Like we have to optimize iterate.

128
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:30,400
So we wanted to learn, hey, we actually have different models.

129
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,960
We built a different one, so we want to compare.

130
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:43,120
So we want to see how they perform between those models, same time how they perform compared to the paywall without the models.

131
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:46,400
So we can make sure we focus on long term success.

132
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:54,960
For the takeaway for this example is the real unlock wasn't just better pricing, it was a better decision design, right?

133
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:58,320
Helping users choose a product they truly fit them.

134
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:15,760
So for your product, you know, any founders or startups out there, even if you don't have a ML team yet, I think maybe the simple way you can do is design three products or tiers for the customers.

135
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:16,400
Why?

136
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:20,880
Because that's a very common or easy way for users to pick from, right?

137
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:25,120
Some want to buy the most expensive one, some just want to buy the cheapest one.

138
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:28,720
And then some of them don't know how to make decisions, they buy the middle one.

139
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:35,920
So that's usually the simplest way for you to design your product here, because actually change how decisions are made.

140
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:42,400
Let's say, David, if you only offer one product, so your decision is, should I buy or not?

141
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:48,320
But if for a day we'll show you three products, now you're thinking, Which one should I get?

142
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:52,160
So you bypass the yes or no, and then so more likely to convert.

143
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:54,320
Yeah, that's fantastic advice.

144
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:56,960
I do wish we all had ML teams.

145
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:03,120
Maybe that's something at RevenueCat we should be working on to help folks build those kind of sophisticated things into their own apps.

146
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:14,480
But anyways, I did want to move on to this idea of monetization unbundling is that not everyone wants the full subscription, not everyone is going to fit into the gold, the platinum.

147
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:17,440
So, yeah, how do you think about unbundling that?

148
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,000
We actually, you probably know, we have some unbundling products already, like Auto, like LinkedIn.

149
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:25,840
Such as like boost, super likes.

150
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:33,440
I think a couple of things you want to think about, like besides one issue you've already highlighted, is not everyone wants to have a subscription, right?

151
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:34,560
Subscription is great.

152
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:44,560
I mean, don't get me wrong, because you get a package, it's much easier than you have to decide which features you want to pick from and then buy that way.

153
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:45,760
It's painful, right?

154
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:51,520
It's easier to buy a whole package and then repeat it, renew it automatically, so you have to think about it twice.

155
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:52,880
But there were some use cases.

156
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:54,000
Think about like boost.

157
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:56,800
I think it's perfect all-a-car product.

158
00:12:57,120 --> 00:12:57,280
Why?

159
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:02,160
Because you don't it depends on when you want to use it, right?

160
00:13:02,480 --> 00:13:05,760
When you don't receive enough likes, so you want to boost yourself.

161
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:10,320
When let's say you swipe during the peak hours, so you want to use that.

162
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:11,360
So that makes sense.

163
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,480
Super likes too, right?

164
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:16,000
Because it's like, well, I don't know how many super likes I'm going to send.

165
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:18,480
It depends on who I see in the app, right?

166
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:25,920
And another use case we decided to try is the travel mode, also AKA passport mode.

167
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:30,080
It allows any users to see anyone globally.

168
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:31,120
So cool.

169
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:36,800
You know, like, let's say you can go to Paris, go to Spain, whatever you want to go based on your needs.

170
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:39,440
It can be basically you want to travel there or you want to just meet people there.

171
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,920
And the thing about that feature is like, not everyone wants it, right?

172
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:47,280
Maybe because it's a very special case for needs for certain people.

173
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:53,680
In that case, that feature is a great candidate as a like a unbundled feature stand alone.

174
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:59,760
So that's how we think about what features make sense to be unbundled and designed for our customers.

175
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:01,520
How does the passport mode work?

176
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,880
Is it a one time purchase for a limited time?

177
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,920
Is it an add on subscription or how does it work exactly?

178
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:14,720
Yes, so historically this feature is part of a subscription, so you have to get a subscription to use it.

179
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:24,800
And then, but like we'll talk about, the challenge for that is for some users, they only want to use it when they plan to travel or to meet people outside of their home city.

180
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:28,480
And then they might not want to buy a subscription, right?

181
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:33,600
Because they just don't want to have that commitment or they don't want to, they don't feel like they need to use other features.

182
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:35,520
So that's a perfect candidate for them.

183
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:37,440
So we tested the features.

184
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:55,120
So that helped us to capture non-subscribers for the users who just want to buy a car for this passport feature and then they can use it for like one day, three days, a week based on the offers we provide and then they can enjoy that benefit to meet people anywhere in the world.

185
00:14:55,600 --> 00:15:01,600
So how have things been going with the passport feature and it being unbundled from the subscription.

186
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:03,280
Have you seen uptake?

187
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:04,000
Yes.

188
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:12,000
Actually, I think it's so fun to talk about this because it was not launch and then success story.

189
00:15:12,080 --> 00:15:12,480
That's it.

190
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:13,200
Not that simple.

191
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:15,840
We learned a few things based on our tests.

192
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:24,000
First thing is when we launched this standalone all car passport feature and then the conversion went crazy.

193
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:24,640
It was great.

194
00:15:25,520 --> 00:15:29,040
However, we noticed like, oh, that hurts our Plus subscription.

195
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:33,840
Because some users decide to only buy this feature now to buy subscription.

196
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:35,520
But that can be a couple of things.

197
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:38,080
So one is, oh, maybe we priced too low.

198
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:40,000
It's too affordable.

199
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,400
And then so it's like no brainer.

200
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:43,600
So users use it.

201
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:52,880
The second thing we did is like, oh, we should increase the price for our card and to see how that would change the conversion and then subscription cannibalization.

202
00:15:53,200 --> 00:16:01,440
So we definitely noticed like the subscription cannibalization reduced as we expected and the conversion reduced a little bit but total revenue incre actually went up.

203
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:02,800
So that was very positive.

204
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:08,000
And then we continued to test because we wanted to minimize the subscription cannibalizations.

205
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:13,280
So and then we increased the price again and then also have upsell on the paywall.

206
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:20,880
Basically, we showed, hey, David, you can buy passport stand alone allocate card, but also you can buy plus subscription.

207
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:24,960
And then we used a very interesting, like a kind of psychology thing.

208
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:33,920
Basically for our seven day passport feature allocate card, the price is the same as seven day plus subscription.

209
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:41,520
So in that case, David, you think like, oh, duh, then the seven day plus subscription is a better deal, right?

210
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,440
